# TODO

## General

- Consider using a centralized logging solution like ELK or Datadog or loki with Grafana for better observability.
- Add as services in denis: n8n, penpot, appflowy, and other open source tools that can be used by the agents and the users.

## New Features

- Projects / Workspaces: group related conversations, files, agents, tools, preferences, and instructions into persistent workspaces for long-running work.
- Deep Research mode: run longer multi-step research workflows with source citations, confidence notes, step traces, and exportable reports. Make it budgeted and steerable from day one: explicit knobs for max sources / max tool calls / wall-clock budget, a plan-first HITL checkpoint (reusing the run takeover interrupt) where the user can prune or redirect branches before execution, per-source trust weighting (domain allow/denylist, prefer-primary-sources toggle), and selectable output templates (executive summary, annotated bibliography, comparison matrix, decision memo).
- Scheduled Tasks: let users create one-off or recurring agent jobs that run later, complete while the user is offline, and notify them with results.
- Artifacts / Canvas: add an editable side workspace for generated reports, markdown docs, code, tables, diagrams, JSON configs, and other reusable outputs.
- Agent Arena (blind compare): send one prompt to two or more agents (or the same agent on different models) side by side with labels hidden, let the user vote, and optionally layer an LLM-judge with a configurable rubric (correctness, completeness, tone, latency, cost) on top of the blind vote. Persist results into a per-agent/per-model ELO leaderboard so picking a default agent or model becomes data-backed instead of vibes. The bridge already runs each inference as an independent Redis-Streams run, so this is a fan-out of two runs plus a side-by-side timeline in the UI.
- Tool RAG (dynamic tool selection): stop sending every tool schema into context as the MCP catalog grows. Embed the MCP manifests and native tool descriptions, retrieve only the top-k tools relevant to the current turn, and lazily expand when the agent asks for more. Hard constraint: the client-computed `enabledTools` list stays the authoritative superset — retrieval narrows within it, never widens it.
- Memory lifecycle (pairs with the pgvector item under Dialogue Bridge): cross-chat memory is only trustworthy if it is scoped, decaying, and correctable. Namespaces per user/workspace/agent so contexts don't bleed into each other; provenance on every memory ("learned from conversation X on date Y"); confidence that decays unless reaffirmed; conflict surfacing when two memories disagree; and a Memory inspector panel in the UI where the user can view, edit, and delete what the system remembers about them.
- Usage & cost dashboard: per-user / per-agent / per-conversation token counts, cost estimates, time-to-first-token, tokens/sec, and tool-call counts. The run event log already carries most of what's needed — add a usage event, aggregate into Postgres, render with the planned shadcn/charts work. Also unlocks per-user quotas later.
- Trust tiers + confirmation gate for irreversible tool calls: classify every tool by reversibility (read / write / irreversible-external) and route irreversible calls (file delete, external send, code execution) through the existing HITL takeover UI as an approval interrupt, governed by a per-agent/per-workspace policy instead of prompt instructions. Must land before the execute-tool/sandbox item under Agents ships.
- Learn from corrections: the message tree already records edits, retries, and which sibling the user kept — mine those as labeled preference signals (which agent/model answer survived, which got retried) to feed the Arena leaderboard and smarter agent/model defaults. Adaptivity for free from data we already store.
- Workflow / automation builder: composable trigger → agent run → action chains ("every morning, run agent X over the new files in workspace Y and notify me with the summary"). Builds directly on Scheduled Tasks; instead of writing a visual editor from scratch, evaluate wiring n8n (already on the General list for Dennis) as the engine, with mAgenticX inference exposed to it as webhook nodes.
- Email integration: connect user mailboxes (IMAP/SMTP; OAuth from day one for Outlook/Office 365 and Gmail — password auth is a dead end there) and expose an email tool surface to agents: read, search, summarize, draft. Build triage policy-first, not vibes-first: declarative rules run before the LLM (VIP sender list → urgent, regex on subject → tag:finance), and the LLM only classifies the ambiguous remainder. Auto-summary and auto-tag run freely; auto-reply drafting is confidence-gated; an actual send always requires human confirmation — route it through the trust-tier confirmation gate above, since email send is the canonical irreversible-external call. Learn from corrections: a user re-tagging a message or deleting a draft is a labeled signal for future triage.
- Notification system + PWA: a unified notification service in the bridge with multiple channels — web push (installable PWA via manifest + service worker), email, and an in-app notification inbox — covering run-complete, scheduled-task results, HITL approval requests, and email-triage urgency alerts. Per-user channel preferences and quiet hours. Scheduled Tasks "complete while the user is offline and notify them" needs this anyway, and the HITL confirmation gate is only usable async if the approval request can reach the user wherever they are.

## Agents

- Update the retrieval process and the whole RAG pipelines so that it will be like an mcp tool calling.
- Add end-to-end file attachment support in inference, routed through the per-conversation filesystem. Bridge: persist uploaded files to `<user>/agents/<slug>/<conv-id>/input/<filename>` (in addition to / instead of the current Postgres blob path). Deep agents: split `/conversation/` into `input/` (read-only — user uploads) and `output/` (read-write — agent-generated artifacts), mark `input/` write-deny via `permissions=[FilesystemPermission(operations=["write"], paths=["/conversation/input/**"], mode="deny")]` (now available — upgraded to `deepagents==0.6.10`, so the old read-only-backend-wrapper workaround is no longer needed), and update Omni's system prompt to direct writes to `/conversation/output/`. LangGraph agents: normalize/parse file parts into the agent state (these agents have no filesystem mount).
- Lock down the per-(user, agent, conversation) workspace so the running agent can never alter its own permissions or mount layout. Today this is implicit (deepagents' filesystem tools expose only ls/read/write/edit/glob/grep — no chmod/chown/mount) but becomes load-bearing the moment we add an execute tool. At that point we also need to resolve the conflict between sandbox isolation and persistent file storage: an ephemeral sandbox per call gives full isolation but loses access to `/conversation/input/` and `/conversation/output/`; mounting the workspace into the sandbox preserves file flow but the sandbox's isolation is then scoped to "everything outside the workspace." Pick a path before shipping execute — likely a gVisor/Firecracker/Daytona-style kernel with `input/` bind-mounted read-only, `output/` read-write, no network by default, dropped caps, no-new-privileges, and hard CPU+memory+time limits. **(Update — now on `deepagents==0.6.10`):** verified that `execute` is config-gated in Omni's `interrupt_on` but **runtime-filtered out** today — the `CompositeBackend` default is `StateBackend`, which is not a `SandboxBackendProtocol`, so deepagents strips the execute tool before the model sees it. There is no active code-execution path; the live risk is a future switch of that default to a shell-capable backend (especially `LocalShellBackend`, which runs commands on the host). The implicit lockdown can now also be made explicit with `permissions=[FilesystemPermission(...)]`.

## Dialogue Bridge

- Add the pgvector in order to have an embedding for each conversation and then we can use this embedding to find the most relevant conversations for a given query, this will be useful for the retrieval process and memory across chats.

## Agentic UI

- Chart can be visualized with [shadcn/charts](https://www.shadcn.io/charts) and the agent can have a custom tool like the todo tool in order to represent the chart and create a custom AGUI event for the interaction with the chart.

## Security

Findings from the full security audit (2026-06-10). Overall posture is strong — opaque HMAC-hashed session tokens (no JWT), constant-time CSRF + trusted-proxy comparison, parameterized SQL, consistent IDOR scoping, enforced deep-agent filesystem isolation, no committed keys/`.env`. The items below are the real action items, ordered by severity. Most cluster around the MCP gateway (the one unauthenticated/plaintext/privileged edge), two production credential mistakes, and inline-preview/header gaps in the UI.

### Critical

- Live Tavily API token committed in cleartext on disk at `src/mcp_gateway/mcp_secret.env:1` (`tvly-dev-...`). Gitignored and absent from git history, but it's the only secret bypassing the Swarm-secrets model and sits in cleartext on every dev machine and on the VM at `/opt/magenticx/mcp_config/`. Rotate the token now (treat the current value as burned), then move it to a Swarm secret mounted via `secrets:` like the others.

### High

- Production Postgres uses `admin:admin` credentials, hardcoded in `src/docker-compose-denis.yaml:104` (DSN at `:159`) — the one prod credential that escaped the secrets model. DB is internal-network-only so blast radius is limited to a container on `backend` or an RCE in bridge/agents, but it's below standard for the store of all user conversations. Fix: strong password as a `magenticx_postgres_password` Swarm secret consumed via `POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE`.
- Vector DB (Chroma) runs with neither TLS nor authentication — `vectordb` service in `src/docker-compose-denis.yaml` (no certs, no auth env) and `src/rag_service/main.py:83` (`chromadb.HttpClient(host, port)`, no `ssl=`; `chroma_api_impl="rest"`). The `rag_service → vectordb` hop is plaintext HTTP and Chroma 0.6.3 has no auth token, so anything on the `backend` overlay can read or write the entire vector store in cleartext (embeddings + retrieved document content). Internal-network-only (`backend` is `internal: true`) bounds the blast radius to a container on `backend` or an RCE in rag_service/agents — but it's the only data store with *neither* control (Postgres has TLS + creds; Redis now uses `rediss://` after the 2026-06-14 work). It's also the last unencrypted inter-service hop besides `agents → mcp_gateway`. Fix: enable Chroma server auth (`CHROMA_SERVER_AUTHN_PROVIDER` + `CHROMA_SERVER_AUTHN_CREDENTIALS`, client sends the token) so the store is at least authenticated like the others; for encryption, front it with a TLS-terminating sidecar (or a Chroma build with native TLS) and point `rag_service` at it over `https` with `ssl=True` + the internal CA.
- MCP gateway URL is fetched with no scheme/host allowlist, over plaintext SSE, with no internal-trust header — `src/agents/utils/mcp_tools.py:151`, `src/agents/core/settings.py:116`. It's the only inter-service hop that isn't mTLS-protected. Anyone able to influence `MCP_GATEWAY_URL` or answer DNS for `mcp_gateway` makes the agents service ingest attacker-controlled tool definitions. Fix: validate scheme/host at settings load, require `https://` + `verify=get_httpx_verify()` in prod, inject `internal_service_headers()` on the gateway connection.
- MCP tool definitions are trusted wholesale — `src/agents/main.py:634`, `src/agents/runtime/base_agent.py:117`. Tools are filtered only by name, never by schema/description/target. Paired with the SSRF above, this is the blast-radius amplifier for prompt injection: a poisoned tool can steer the model into exfiltrating conversation/file data to an attacker URL. Fix: enforce the existing `_TOOL_SERVER_OVERRIDES` map (pin expected server per tool) instead of only labeling.
- Cross-table data access in rag_service — `src/rag_service/main.py:58`. `_validate_sql_references_table` only checks the path-param table name appears somewhere in the SQL; it does not restrict the query to it. Confirmed by test: `SELECT salaries.pay FROM salaries CROSS JOIN sales` scoped to `/excel/sales/` returns the other workbook's data. Fix: parse the SQL and assert the only base tables referenced equal the path table, or give each table its own isolated connection/catalog.
- PDF preview iframe has no `sandbox` attribute — `src/agentic_ui/src/components/chat/attachment_preview_parts/PdfPreview.tsx:21` (contrast `DocxPreview.tsx:15`, which does it right). It renders a same-origin attachment URL, so if the bridge serves stored bytes with a `text/html` content-type this is live same-origin stored XSS (full access to the CSRF cookie + API-as-victim). Fix: add `sandbox`, and have the backend force `Content-Type: application/pdf` on the preview endpoint.

### Medium

- Stored-XSS via unrestricted MIME on the public, unauthenticated blob endpoint — `src/dialogue_bridge/router/attachments.py:84`. The preview token is bound only to `blob_id` (no mime/user binding) and `/public/{token}` serves any mime inline with no CSP. An authenticated user can upload a `text/html` attachment and serve attacker HTML inline on the app origin. Fix: allow-list Office mimes at token issuance, bind the mime into the signed token, force a safe response type + CSP on the public response.
- rag_service SQL validator is a denylist and bypassable — `src/rag_service/main.py:34`. Comment-splitting (`read/**/_csv`) and dozens of unlisted IO/introspection functions pass it. Not exploitable today only because DuckDB's `enable_external_access=false` engine config hard-blocks filesystem access (verified), but the regex is the wrong layer to lean on. Fix: convert to an allowlist (only `SELECT`/`WITH`, known constructs) and strip comments before matching; keep the engine config as the load-bearing control.
- nginx public-blob block missing `X-Frame-Options`/`Referrer-Policy`/CSP — `src/agentic_ui/nginx.conf.template:77`. (The original `/api/` + `/inference/runs/` finding was a false alarm — verified live: those declare no `add_header`, so they correctly inherit all five server-level headers. Only `/api/v1/attachments/public/` loses them, by declaring its own CORS headers.) Subsumed by the public-blob-endpoint item above (force a safe type + CSP there); consider an included headers snippet to avoid the `add_header` inheritance footgun.- TLS entrypoints silently fall back to plaintext if certs are missing/unreadable — `src/tls/entrypoint-tls.sh:20` (and the nginx/postgres variants). A prod service should never silently downgrade onto the backend network. Fix: fail closed in production (e.g. honor a `REQUIRE_TLS=true` flag and `exit 1`).
- MCP gateway runs `privileged: true` (dind) — `src/docker-compose-denis-mcp.yaml:6`. Container-escape-to-host risk on the shared VM, mitigated by network isolation (only on the `mcp_gateway` overlay, no host ports). Fix: pin MCP server images by digest in the catalog, document the catalog as a trust boundary, and explore rootless-dind. Also carries the residual from the High batch: the dind image cannot terminate TLS, so agents→gateway stays plaintext HTTP on the overlay (host-allowlisted + trust header, but not encrypted) — revisit if the gateway ever gains TLS support or moves behind an mTLS sidecar.

### Low / hardening

- CSP allows `'unsafe-inline'` for scripts — `src/agentic_ui/nginx.conf.template:34`. Move to a nonce/hash-based CSP (Vite bundles are hashable) and drop `'unsafe-inline'` for `script-src`.
